### What is the Well-Architected Framework?

The framework is a structured way to ask "is this architecture good, and where are its risks?" Each pillar is a lens with specific design principles and guiding questions. AWS publishes whitepapers for each pillar and provides the Well-Architected Tool to run reviews as structured questionnaires.

```java
// Well-Architected Framework overview
package com.bank.architecture;

import java.util.List;

public class WellArchitectedPillars {
    public static final List<String> PILLARS = List.of(
        "1. Operational Excellence",
        "2. Security",
        "3. Reliability",
        "4. Performance Efficiency",
        "5. Cost Optimization",
        "6. Sustainability"
    );

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("AWS Well-Architected Framework: 6 Pillars");
        PILLARS.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### What is Operational Excellence?

Operational Excellence covers running systems well and improving them continuously. It means infrastructure as code so changes are repeatable and version-controlled, observability so you can see what the system is doing, runbooks and automated responses to events, and treating operations as code you can test.

```java
// Operational Excellence: infrastructure as code
package com.bank.architecture;

public class OperationalExcellenceCheck {
    public static void audit() {
        System.out.println("OE-1: All infrastructure defined in CloudFormation/CDK? ");
        System.out.println("OE-2: CloudWatch dashboards for key business metrics? ");
        System.out.println("OE-3: Automated alarms and runbooks for common failures? ");
        System.out.println("OE-4: CI/CD pipeline with automated testing and rollback? ");
        System.out.println("OE-5: Post-incident reviews conducted and actions tracked? ");
    }
}
```

### What is the Security pillar?

Security covers protecting information and systems. It means strong identity and access management with least privilege, encryption in transit and at rest, traceability through logging and CloudTrail, protection at every layer, and preparing for incidents.

```java
// Security pillar checklist
package com.bank.architecture;

public class SecurityPillarCheck {
    public static void audit() {
        System.out.println("SEC-1: IAM least privilege for all roles and policies? ");
        System.out.println("SEC-2: Encryption at rest (KMS) and in transit (TLS)? ");
        System.out.println("SEC-3: CloudTrail enabled in all regions? ");
        System.out.println("SEC-4: Security groups: no 0.0.0.0/0 on sensitive ports? ");
        System.out.println("SEC-5: Incident response plan documented and tested? ");
    }
}
```

### What is the Reliability pillar?

Reliability covers performing correctly and recovering from failure. It means designing across Availability Zones, automatic recovery through health checks and failover, backups with tested restoration, handling change safely, and scaling to meet demand.

```java
// Reliability pillar checklist
package com.bank.architecture;

public class ReliabilityPillarCheck {
    public static void audit() {
        System.out.println("REL-1: Multi-AZ deployment for database and application? ");
        System.out.println("REL-2: Health checks with automatic replacement of unhealthy? ");
        System.out.println("REL-3: Automated backups with tested restoration procedure? ");
        System.out.println("REL-4: Auto-scaling configured for variable demand? ");
        System.out.println("REL-5: DR strategy defined and game-day tested? ");
    }
}
```

### What is Performance Efficiency?

Performance Efficiency covers using resources efficiently as demand and technology change. It means choosing the right resource types and sizes, scaling elastically, using the right data store for each workload, and measuring before optimizing.

```java
// Performance Efficiency checklist
package com.bank.architecture;

public class PerformanceEfficiencyCheck {
    public static void audit() {
        System.out.println("PE-1: Instance types selected based on workload profile? ");
        System.out.println("PE-2: Auto-scaling responding to actual demand metrics? ");
        System.out.println("PE-3: Database type matches access pattern (SQL vs NoSQL)? ");
        System.out.println("PE-4: CDN (CloudFront) for static content? ");
        System.out.println("PE-5: Load testing done before production launch? ");
    }
}
```

### What are Cost Optimization and Sustainability?

Cost Optimization means avoiding unnecessary spend: matching supply to demand, using Savings Plans or Reserved Instances for steady workloads, turning off idle environments, and tagging for cost attribution. Sustainability means minimizing environmental impact: choosing efficient regions and instance types, right-sizing, and reducing waste.

```java
// Cost Optimization and Sustainability checklists
package com.bank.architecture;

public class CostAndSustainabilityCheck {
    public static void audit() {
        System.out.println("COST-1: Savings Plans for predictable baseline workloads? ");
        System.out.println("COST-2: Dev/test environments shut down nights and weekends? ");
        System.out.println("COST-3: All resources tagged for cost attribution? ");
        System.out.println("COST-4: Unattached EBS volumes and idle LBs cleaned up? ");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("SUS-1: Right-sized instances (not over-provisioned)? ");
        System.out.println("SUS-2: S3 lifecycle policies for aging data? ");
    }
}
```

### Why review before production?

The pillars involve trade-offs, and the framework makes them explicit. More reliability often costs more; the fastest option is not always the cheapest. Reviewing before production forces these trade-offs into the open while they are still cheap to change. Fixing a single-AZ database in a design document is trivial; discovering it during a production outage is a disaster.

```java
// Self-assessment: score each pillar, surface the weakest
package com.bank.architecture;

import java.util.List;

public class WellArchitectedReview {
    record PillarScore(String pillar, int score, String topRisk) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<PillarScore> review = List.of(
            new PillarScore("Reliability", 2, "Single-AZ RDS, no failover"),
            new PillarScore("Security", 4, "Block Public Access enabled"),
            new PillarScore("Cost Optimization", 2, "No cost tags, idle envs running 24/7"),
            new PillarScore("Operational Excellence", 3, "Missing runbooks for top failures"),
            new PillarScore("Performance Efficiency", 4, "Auto-scaling configured"),
            new PillarScore("Sustainability", 3, "No lifecycle policies yet")
        );
        System.out.println("Well-Architected Review Results:");
        review.forEach(p -> System.out.println(
            "  " + p.pillar() + ": " + p.score() + "/5 - Risk: " + p.topRisk()));
    }
}
```

### How does the Well-Architected Tool help?

AWS provides the Well-Architected Tool to run reviews as structured questionnaires. You answer questions for each pillar, the tool identifies high-risk issues and medium-risk items, and it generates an improvement plan with milestones and links to remediation guidance.

```java
// Using the Well-Architected Tool
package com.bank.architecture;

public class WellArchitectedToolUsage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Create a workload in the WA Tool");
        System.out.println("2. Answer questions for each of the 6 pillars");
        System.out.println("3. Tool identifies HRI (high risk) and MRI (medium risk) items");
        System.out.println("4. Generate improvement plan with milestones");
        System.out.println("5. Track remediation to closure");
        System.out.println("6. Re-review periodically as architecture evolves");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The framework is not a one-time exercise. Architectures evolve, and the review should be repeated periodically and after major changes. Senior engineers use the framework to communicate trade-offs to stakeholders: "we can improve reliability by adding Multi-AZ, which costs X more per month, and here is the risk if we do not." The framework's real value is making these conversations structured and evidence-based.
